<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mithridates VI of Pontus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">135–<a href="/years/63-bce">63 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Mithridates the Great, Mithridates VI Eupator</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mithridates VI Eupator was king of Pontus from about 120 BCE and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s most persistent enemy in the east. He built an empire around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, taking the Bosporan Kingdom and Lesser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, and in 88 BCE ordered the massacre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in Asia. He fought three wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>, Lucullus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>, and died in 63 BCE after his final defeat.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus.jpg" alt="Mithridates VI of Pontus" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mithridates_VI_Louvre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 88 BCE, under the leadership of Athenion and then Aristion, Athens sided with <b>Mithridates VI of Pontus</b> against Rome.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Mithridates VI of Pontus</b> the kingdom was governed by his son Machares as viceroy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bosporan-kingdom">Bosporan Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…with Rome, and according to Tacitus, envoys reminded the Senate in 53 CE of the help their city had given to Roman commanders in wars against the Macedonian kings, Antiochus III, Aristonicus, the pirates and <b>Mithridates VI of Pontus</b>; Claudius responded by remitting their tribute for five years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mithridates VI of Pontus</b>, who suspected the Chians of favouring Rome, imposed a heavy fine in 86 BCE and then had his general Zenobius deport the population towards the Black Sea; according to Memnon of Heraclea, many were freed on the way by the people of Heraclea Pontica.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Rome conquered the empire in 69 BCE, and the defeat of Armenia's ally <b>Mithridates VI of Pontus</b> by Pompey at the Battle of the Lycus made the kingdom a Roman client in 66 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mithridates VI of Pontus</b> quelled an uprising in the region in 83 BCE, and Pompey occupied Colchis after Mithridates' defeat in 65 BCE, capturing the local chief Olthaces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-colchis">Kingdom of Colchis · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sinop, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panticapaeum">Panticapaeum</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.03,35.15&amp;year=-89" aria-label="Sinop on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5404 668 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mithridates VI of Pontus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5854 938h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5854 938h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.03,35.15&amp;year=-89">What was here in 89 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
